index 0000000..66cdb5f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/steps/10-cloud-worker.md
@@ -0,0 +1,201 @@
+# Run one experiment on a cloud worker
+
+The previous step copied locally calculated experiments to Google Cloud Storage.
+This step moves the calculation itself onto a temporary cloud computer. One
+Google Cloud Run Job starts Flowstate, runs one small CPU experiment, saves its
+evidence to the existing bucket, and exits.
+
+For the first example, the input is `examples/burgers.json`: a Burgers simulation
+with 64 spatial points, 200 time steps, viscosity `0.05`, and a sine-shaped
+starting pattern. The output is an experiment record, numerical arrays,
+measurements, and checksums that you can download and inspect locally.
+
+```text
+Local source snapshot + locked Python dependencies
+                  |
+              Cloud Build
+                  | builds a container image
+            Artifact Registry
+                  | stores the runnable image
+             Cloud Run Job
+                  | starts one container
+          read experiment JSON settings
+                  |
+          solver -> temporary local lake
+                  |
+          verify artifacts -> GCS upload
+                  |
+ gs://flowstate-codex/flowstate/experiments/<id>/manifest.json
+                  |
+          download -> verify -> local viewer
+```
+
+Cloud Run supplies the computer. Artifact Registry stores the packaged program.
+GCS stores the experiment evidence after calculation. Cloud Build turns the
+selected source files into that runnable package. These services have different
+jobs; uploading a JSON file to GCS alone does not start a simulation.
+
+## Starting point and implementation choices
+
+The existing engine already knows how to validate a configuration, run a solver,
+capture provenance, and publish an immutable local experiment. The GCS adapter
+already knows how to verify and copy those artifacts. The cloud worker connects
+these two paths instead of implementing another solver or storage format.
+
+The first deployment is intentionally bounded:
+
+| Setting | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| Project | `flowstate-510320` |
+| Region | `us-east1` |
+| Experiment bucket | `flowstate-codex` |
+| Object prefix | `flowstate/` |
+| Runtime identity | `flowstate@flowstate-510320.iam.gserviceaccount.com` |
+| Build identity | `flowstate-builder@flowstate-510320.iam.gserviceaccount.com` |
+| Tasks per execution / parallel tasks | `1` / `1` |
+| CPU / memory | `1` CPU / `1 GiB` |
+| Task timeout / automatic retries | `300` seconds / `0` |
+| Maximum grid size | `128`, or `65` for Darcy |
+| Maximum time steps | `10,000` |
+
+The worker validates its workload before calculation. The grid and step limits
+are program safeguards, while the memory and timeout limits are enforced by the
+cloud job. The limits do not guarantee that every permitted configuration will
+finish within five minutes. Start with the small Burgers example.
+
+This is a manually started, single-experiment worker. The local SQLite queue
+remains a local queue. There is no distributed scheduler, GPU training, or
+automatic parameter sweep in this milestone. Cloud Run Jobs execute their tasks
+and exit rather than serving an HTTP endpoint. [Cloud Run Jobs documentation](https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/create-jobs).
+
+## What happens to the data
+
+1. **Extract settings:** The worker reads one JSON configuration included in the
+   image. Configuration validation fills in supported defaults and rejects
+   invalid settings. The worker applies its additional resource limits.
+2. **Calculate:** `engine.run_experiment()` selects the equation's solver. For
+   Burgers, the solver repeatedly calculates the next state from the current
+   state and the configured time step. It keeps the requested saved frames.
+3. **Transform:** The engine derives summary measurements from numerical
+   diagnostics. `Lake.write()` publishes the detailed `record.json`, a flattened
+   `metadata.parquet` row, chunked `fields.zarr` arrays where available, and a
+   `manifest.json` containing artifact checksums.
+4. **Load:** `gcs_store.upload_experiment()` passes the verified local experiment
+   to the shared artifact mirror. Its upload loop copies the artifact files to
+   GCS and publishes the completion manifest last.
+5. **Retrieve:** The existing `flowstate gcs download` command restores the same
+   bytes into a local lake. The local catalog queries its Parquet metadata, and
+   the dashboard can display the restored experiment.
+
+The calculation's temporary lake lives inside the running container. It is not
+durable storage. Once the container disappears, the GCS objects are the durable
+copy. A container stopped before the completion manifest is uploaded may leave
+no complete remote experiment.
+
+The source build also produces an image, build logs, and a deployment report.
+Those are deployment evidence; they are separate from the numerical arrays and
+experiment metadata.
+
+## Functions and loops to read
+
+| Code | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `src/flowstate/cli.py` | Parses the `cloud-worker` command and its configuration/storage arguments. |
+| `src/flowstate/cloud_worker.py` | Validates the bounded job, calls the existing engine, uploads its evidence, and reports the outcome. |
+| `src/flowstate/engine.py`, `run_experiment()` | Normalizes settings, creates the experiment identity, runs the solver, and publishes success or failure evidence. |
+| `src/flowstate/numerics.py`, `solve()` | Selects Burgers, Navier–Stokes, or Darcy calculations. |
+| `src/flowstate/streaming.py`, `ZarrFrameSink` | Writes saved time-dependent fields incrementally instead of keeping every field in memory. |
+| `src/flowstate/lake.py`, `Lake.write()` | Converts the result and record into the local experiment's files. |
+| `src/flowstate/artifact_mirror.py`, `upload()` | Loops over the artifact list, checks each file, and publishes the remote completion manifest last. |
+| `src/flowstate/gcs_store.py`, `_client()` | Uses Application Default Credentials to obtain the attached runtime identity. |
+
+There are two main kinds of repetition. The solver's time-step loop generates
+numerical data. The artifact mirror's file loop transfers the already generated
+data. Darcy is a steady-state calculation, so it produces one solved state
+rather than a time-step history. The worker does not loop forever waiting for
+more experiments: one invocation handles one configuration and exits.
+
+## Authentication and provenance
+
+Cloud Run uses the attached `flowstate` service account. The Python Google SDK
+obtains short-lived credentials through Application Default Credentials (ADC).
+No browser login runs inside the container, and no private key is copied into
+the image. The runtime account retains the bucket's Object Creator and Object
+Viewer access used in the previous step.
+
+Building is a separate operation performed by `flowstate-builder`. The build
+identity needs access to build inputs, image publication, and build logs. The
+runtime identity needs access to experiment objects. Keeping these identities
+separate makes their permissions understandable and independently adjustable.
+
+The image contains a snapshot of local source selected for the build. It does
+not automatically pull the latest GitHub branch whenever the job runs. Code or
+example changes require a new build and deployment before a later execution
+uses them.
+
+An experiment's `source_sha256` hashes the installed Python source. It identifies
+the actual program files used by the worker. The container may not contain Git
+metadata, so `git_commit` can be `null`. `FLOWSTATE_BUILD_COMMIT` is a declared
+source commit supplied when building the image; it is not an independent proof
+that the local source had no uncommitted changes. The deployment report records
+the built image digest, which identifies the deployed container image. Read
+these records together when reproducing a cloud run.
+
+## Failures and repetition
+
+A recorded numerical failure is useful evidence. If the engine records one,
+the worker still uploads its failure record and checksums, then exits with a
+nonzero status so the cloud execution is visibly failed. Failed calculations
+may have no field arrays. An upload failure also fails the worker; it must not
+be reported as a successful archived experiment or as a physical anomaly.
+
+The job is configured with zero automatic retries. If a process is terminated
+by its time limit or memory limit, its temporary lake may be lost. There is no
+mid-simulation checkpoint recovery. A new execution starts the calculation
+again; matching immutable artifacts can be reused by the mirror only when their
+bytes agree. An incomplete upload is not complete until its manifest exists.
+
+Do not treat a cloud execution name as an experiment ID. Execution names locate
+the job and logs. The worker's output supplies the experiment ID used to restore
+the scientific evidence.
+
+## Inspect an execution and retrieve the evidence
+
+After deployment, use the Cloud Run Jobs page in project `flowstate-510320` to
+open the job, select its execution, and read the logs. The logs show the worker's
+structured output and experiment ID. A green cloud execution alone does not
+replace verification of the downloaded artifacts.
+
+For a command-line log view, replace `JOB_NAME` with the deployed job name:
+
+```powershell
+gcloud run jobs logs read JOB_NAME --project=flowstate-510320 --region=us-east1 --limit=50
+```
+
+This command reads job logs and does not start a new calculation. [Google Cloud CLI reference](https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/run/jobs/logs/read).
+
+Copy the reported experiment ID into the following commands. Run them from the
+repository root with the existing local GCS authentication:
+
+```powershell
+uv run --no-sync flowstate --lake data/cloud-restored gcs download EXPERIMENT_ID flowstate-codex --prefix flowstate/ --project flowstate-510320
+uv run --no-sync flowstate --lake data/cloud-restored verify EXPERIMENT_ID
+uv run --no-sync flowstate --lake data/cloud-restored show EXPERIMENT_ID
+uv run --no-sync flowstate --lake data/cloud-restored dashboard outputs/cloud-worker.html
+Start-Process outputs/cloud-worker.html
+```
+
+Choose a new dashboard filename if that export already exists. Downloading and
+viewing evidence does not rerun the simulation.
+
+## What verifies this milestone
+
+Local tests should cover workload validation, engine-to-upload handoff, storage
+errors, and preservation of failed numerical evidence. Those checks exercise
+the program behavior without proving that a cloud deployment works.
+
+A live validation requires a successful image build, a completed cloud
+execution, a remote completion manifest, and a download whose files pass
+`flowstate verify`. Record the build/image, execution name, experiment ID, and
+verification result after those steps occur. This chapter does not itself claim
+that a live execution has succeeded.
